      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML Certificate of Conformity.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Certificate number: R76/2006-GB1-12.05, Revision 2\n- Scheme: This is an MAA certificate (OIML MAA - Mutual Acceptance Arrangement). The scheme field is \"A|B\" per schema... Hmm, the schema says scheme (A|B). This is an MAA certificate — the logo says \"MUTUAL ACCEPTANCE ARRANGEMENT\". MAA certificates typically have scheme A or B. The document doesn't explicitly say \"MAA certificate scheme A\" or similar. Actually the footer says \"OIML R76 MAA Rev. 2\". It doesn't state scheme A or B explicitly in the fragment. I should probably omit scheme since not stated, or... The rules say don't fabricate. So omit scheme.\n\n- project_number: TS1201/0041 (Reference №)\n- page_total: 5 (\"This certificate includes 5 pages\")\n- member_state: United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\n- date_issued: 22 August 2014 → 2014-08-22\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present explicitly.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: National Measurement Office\n- address_lines: Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\n- person_responsible: Paul Dixon – Director, Certification Services. Actually person_title: Director, Certification Services; person_responsible: Paul Dixon.\n- phone: +44 (0)20 8943 7272\n- website: www.bis.gov.uk/nmo\n- Signatory: G Stones — that's the signatory of the certificate; person_responsible is Paul Dixon. Hmm. The signatory is G Stones. I'll keep person_responsible as Paul Dixon with title.\n\nApplicants:\n- Avery Weigh-Tronix, Foundry Lane, Smethwick, West Midlands B66 2LP, United Kingdom\n\nManufacturers:\n- \"The applicant\" — the manufacturer is the applicant. Should I record a manufacturer entry? The rule says parse correctly. I could omit manufacturers[] since it just says \"the applicant\" — but to be faithful, maybe include manufacturer with name \"Avery Weigh-Tronix\" same address? Or record as null? I think it's cleaner to note manufacturers same as applicant. Hmm. Rule 8: don't fabricate. The document says \"Manufacturer: The applicant\". So the manufacturer is Avery Weigh-Tronix. I could include manufacturers[] with the applicant's details, or omit. I'll include it with name \"Avery Weigh-Tronix\" and same address since it says manufacturer is the applicant. Actually, to avoid fabrication, maybe I should record manufacturers entry referencing applicant. Let me include: {\"name\": \"Avery Weigh-Tronix\", \"address_lines\": [...]} — since \"The applicant\" resolves to Avery Weigh-Tronix. That seems reasonable.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: This is a Non-Automatic Weighing Instrument (checkweigher). Category options: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... For R76, category would be \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" or \"Checkweigher\". The document says \"ZQ375 Checkweigher\" and \"Non-Automatic Weighing Instruments\". I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument (checkweigher)\" or just \"Checkweigher\". Let me use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" — hmm, schema examples give instrument categories. I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument (checkweigher)\". Actually simpler: \"Checkweigher\". Let me think — the schema says category # \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... The \"...\" implies flexibility. I'll go with \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n- type_designations: [\"ZQ375\"] — family includes Torsion base and Diamond base models. Main features mention ZQ375 Indicating Device, bases BSF/BSG Torsion, BS Diamond.\n- module_designation: This is a complete instrument, not a module. \"Not applicable\"? The schema says \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. It's a complete NAWI, so maybe null or \"Not applicable\". I'll put null since it's not stated. Hmm, or \"Not applicable\" because it's a complete instrument. I'll go with null to be safe... Actually the cert is for a complete instrument so module_designation is not applicable. I'll use null (omit).\n- description: \"This family of instruments is designated the ZQ375 Checkweigher and comprises the Torsion base and Diamond base models. The instruments are Class III, mains or battery-powered, self-indicating, Non-Automatic Weighing Instruments, and are not designed for direct sales to the public.\"\n\nModel family:\n- family_name: \"ZQ375 Checkweigher\"\n- models: Torsion base and Diamond base models. The tables give base model variants: BSF-99-3, BSF-99-6 (two entries with different load cells/max), BSF-1214-15 (two entries), BSF-1214-30, BSF-1214-45, BSF-1214-60, BSG-99-6 (two), BSG-1214-15 (two), BSG-1214-30, BS-1824-45 (two).\n\nThe base model tables are config-level / model-level matrices. Each table has columns: Base Model, Load Cell Model, Max (kg), Min (g), e = (g), T ≤ (kg), Emax (kg).\n\nNote duplicate column headers (BSF-99-6 appears twice, BSF-1214-15 twice, etc.) representing different configurations (different Max with same base model). These are configurations within base models.\n\nHow to model? Options:\n1. matrix_tables[]: one per base table, preserving rows.\n2. model_family.models: one entry per variant (base model + max combination).\n3. characteristics.model_level: attributes per variant.\n4. characteristics.config_level: axis = base model/configuration.\n\nI think matrix_tables is the best fit for these multi-dim tables, plus model_family models per variant. The condition distinguishes by \"Base Model\" — models vary by base model and load cell configuration.\n\nLet me define model IDs: e.g., \"BSF-99-3\", \"BSF-99-6 (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg, Max 3 kg)\"... that's getting complicated. Maybe simpler: model_id per base-model variant: e.g., \"BSF-99-3\", \"BSF-99-6 [Max 3 kg]\", \"BSF-99-6 [Max 6 kg]\", etc. Or just use the table rows as matrix_tables and set models as base models: BSF-99, BSF-1214, BSG-99, BSG-1214, BS-1824. Plus the indicator ZQ375.\n\nThe ZQ375 family: Torsion base (BSF/BSG) and Diamond base (BS) models. So model family models could be: \"Torsion base (BSF/BSG)\" and \"Diamond base (BS)\". But the tables give finer detail.\n\nI'll do:\n- model_family: family_name \"ZQ375 Checkweigher\", models: list of variants based on tables. Each variant model_id like \"BSF-99-3\", \"BSF-99-6 (Max 3 kg)\", ... Hmm, duplicated base model names need disambiguation. Let me use model_id as base model + Max: e.g. \"BSF-99-3\", \"BSF-99-6/Max 3 kg\"... \n\nAlternatively, treat each table as matrix_table and don't enumerate models in model_family beyond the two base families (Torsion, Diamond). The schema says model_family include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. It covers many variants. I'll enumerate.\n\nLet me build matrix_tables with rows keyed by base model and configuration:\n\nTable 1: BSF-99 Torsion base (HBM PW15AHC3MR load cell)\nrows:\n- {base_model: \"BSF-99-3\", load_cell_model: \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", max: 3, min: 20, e: 1, t_max: 3, emax: 10} units: max kg, min g, e g, t kg, emax kg\n- {base_model: \"BSF-99-6\", load_cell_model: \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", max: 6, min: 40, e: 2, t_max: 6, emax: 10}\n- {base_model: \"BSF-99-6\", load_cell_model: \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", max: 6, min: 40, e: 2, t_max: 6, emax: 20}\n\nTable 2: BSF-1214:\n- BSF-1214-15, PW15AHC3MR/20 kg, max 6, min 40, e 2, T 6, Emax 20\n- BSF-1214-15, PW15AHC3MR/20 kg, max 15, min 100, e 5, T 15, Emax 20\n- BSF-1214-30, PW15AHC3MR/50 kg, max 30, min 200, e 10, T 30, Emax 50\n- BSF-1214-45, PW15AHC3MR/100 kg, max 45, min 400, e 20, T 45, Emax 100\n- BSF-1214-60, PW15AHC3MR/100 kg, max 60, min 400, e 20, T 60, Emax 100\n\nNote: OCR shows \"PW15AHC3 MR/100 kg\" with odd spacing — normalize to \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\". The load cell model is PW15AHC3MR with capacity suffix /20 kg etc. I'll normalize as \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\" etc.\n\nTable 3: BSG-99 (Vishay 1130):\n- BSG-99-6, 1130-10 kg, max 3, min 20, e 1, T 3, Emax 10\n- BSG-99-6, 1130-10 kg, max 6, min 40, e 2, T 6, Emax 10\n\nLoad cell model written \"1 130-10 kg\" → normalize to \"1130-10 kg\" (Vishay model 1130). Good.\n\nTable 4: BSG-1214:\n- BSG-1214-15, 1130-20 kg, max 6, min 40, e 2, T 6, Emax 20\n- BSG-1214-15, 1130-20 kg, max 15, min 100, e 5, T 15, Emax 20\n- BSG-1214-30, 1130-50 kg, max 30, min 200, e 10, T 30, Emax 50\n\nTable 5: 1824 Diamond base (Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60):\n- BS-1824-45, FLS R60 59.5 kg, max 30, min 200, e 10, T 30, Emax 59.5\n- BS-1824-45, FLS R60 59.5 kg, max 45, min 200, e 10, T 45, Emax 59.5\n\nNote the heading says \"1824 Diamond base (with Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60 load cell)\". Table name \"1824 Diamond base\". The column headers: Base Model, Load Cell Model, Max (kg), Min (g), e = (g), T ≤ (kg), Emax (kg).\n\nComponents: The cert names sub-assemblies:\n- ZQ375 Indicating Device (described in R76/2006-GB1-12.04) — role: indicating device\n- BSF / BSG Torsion or BS Diamond base stainless steel load receptor — bases\n- Load cells: HBM PW15AHC3MR, Vishay 1130, Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS — role: load cell, alternatives OR\n- Optional ZQ-BAT battery box\n- Optional ZQ-OPTO interface box (with or without beacon assembly)\n- Stainless Steel Column\n\nComponents array:\n- {role: \"indicating_device\", type_designations: [\"ZQ375 Indicating Device\"], ...} — maybe note described in R76/2006-GB1-12.04.\n- {role: \"load_receptor\", type_designations: [\"BSF\", \"BSG\", \"BS\"], characteristics?} Torsion base stainless steel load receptor; Diamond base stainless steel load receptor.\n- {role: \"load_cell\", type_designations: [\"HBM PW15AHC3MR\", \"Vishay 1130\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS\"], alternatives: \"OR\"}\n- {role: \"battery_box\", type_designations: [\"ZQ-BAT\"], optional}\n- {role: \"interface_box\", type_designations: [\"ZQ-OPTO\"], optional, \"with or without beacon assembly\"}\n\nDevices list — could be captured in description or characteristics? There's no explicit schema slot for devices. I could add to