scheme: null, accuracy_classes: [\"III\"] (class [III]). Edition \"2006(E)\" → 2006 int.\n\nTest reports:\n- pattern evaluation report P01332, 13 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (pattern evaluation report). id: \"P01332\", pages: 13, role: \"type_evaluation_report\". date not given.\n\nRevision history:\n- revision: \"2\", date: 22 August 2014, changes: \"This revision replaces earlier versions of this certificate.\" Revision 2 current. Earlier versions unknown. So revision_history: [{revision: \"2\", date: \"2014-08-22\", changes: \"This revision replaces earlier versions of this certificate.\"}]\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05\"\n- scheme: not stated (MAA cert but scheme A/B not specified) — omit.\n- project_number: \"TS1201/0041\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\"\n- date_issued: \"2014-08-22\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present — omit.\n\nShould revision go into certificate number? The number field: \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05\" and revision 2. Schema has no revision field at certificate level; revision_history covers it. Maybe include revision in number? I'll keep number clean and add revision_history.\n\nNow model_family models enumeration. Let me define models as the distinct configurations from the tables:\n\nBSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg), BSF-99-6 (Max 3 kg), BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC 20 kg), BSF-1214-15 (Max 6 kg), BSF-1214-15 (Max 15 kg), BSF-1214-30, BSF-1214-45, BSF-1214-60, BSG-99-6 (Max 3 kg), BSG-99-6 (Max 6 kg), BSG-1214-15 (Max 6 kg), BSG-1214-15 (Max 15 kg), BSG-1214-30, BS-1824-45 (Max 30 kg), BS-1824-45 (Max 45 kg).\n\nHmm — the duplicate base model entries with same Max but different load cell (BSF-99-6 with 10 kg LC vs 20 kg LC — both Max 6 kg, differ in Emax). So model_id should incorporate load cell capacity.\n\nMaybe simpler: model_id per table row: e.g. \"BSF-99-3 / PW15AHC3MR/10 kg / Max 3 kg\". That's verbose. Alternative: use row index.\n\nAlternatively treat base models as the models (BSF-99, BSF-1214, BSG-99, BSG-1214, BS-1824) and the variants as configurations along axis \"base_model_configuration\". That fits config_level.\n\nGiven complexity, I'll do both: model_family.models as base model families with the Torsion/Diamond split... Actually let me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"ZQ375 Checkweigher\"\n- models: \n  - {model_id: \"Torsion base (BSF/BSG)\", label: \"Torsion base models\", attributes: {load_cells: [\"HBM PW15AHC3MR\", \"Vishay 1130\"]}}\n  - {model_id: \"Diamond base (BS)\", label: \"Diamond base model\", attributes: {load_cells: [\"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS\"]}}\n\nAnd then matrix_tables capture the full detail, and characteristics.config_level entries for max/min/e per base model? That's redundant with matrix_tables.\n\nHmm. The R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level entries. For consistency, I could add model_level entries for the varying attributes across all 15 variants. But 15 variants × 5 attributes is heavy. The matrix_tables preserve the tables fully; model_level could summarize per base model variant.\n\nGiven the tables duplicate base model names, the cleanest model IDs are the full row combos. Let me generate 15 model_ids like:\n1. \"BSF-99-3 (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg, Max 3 kg)\" — too verbose.\n\nAlternative: since each table row is uniquely identified by base model + load cell capacity + Max, use e.g. \"BSF-99-3\", \"BSF-99-6/10\", ... hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: perhaps use config_level with axis \"base_model\" and conditions like \"BSF-99 (PW15AHC3MR/10 kg)\". Then values {condition: \"BSF-99-3\", value: 3} for max...\n\nActually I think the most faithful approach: matrix_tables (5 tables, rows as objects) + model_level entries where the model identifier is a composite string. Let me define model_id as \"<base model> / <load cell capacity> / Max <max> kg\"? Still verbose but unambiguous. Or shorter: keep base model and add suffix when duplicated.\n\nLet me enumerate distinct variants:\n\nBSF-99 table:\n1. BSF-99-3 | PW15AHC3MR/10 kg | Max 3\n2. BSF-99-6 | PW15AHC3MR/10 kg | Max 6\n3. BSF-99-6 | PW15AHC3MR/20 kg | Max 6\n\nBSF-1214:\n4. BSF-1214-15 | PW15AHC3MR/20 kg | Max 6\n5. BSF-1214-15 | PW15AHC3MR/20 kg | Max 15\n6. BSF-1214-30 | PW15AHC3MR/50 kg | Max 30\n7. BSF-1214-45 | PW15AHC3MR/100 kg | Max 45\n8. BSF-1214-60 | PW15AHC3MR/100 kg | Max 60\n\nBSG-99:\n9. BSG-99-6 | 1130-10 kg | Max 3\n10. BSG-99-6 | 1130-10 kg | Max 6\n\nBSG-1214:\n11. BSG-1214-15 | 1130-20 kg | Max 6\n12. BSG-1214-15 | 1130-20 kg | Max 15\n13. BSG-1214-30 | 1130-50 kg | Max 30\n\nBS-1824:\n14. BS-1824-45 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 30\n15. BS-1824-45 | FLS R60 59.5 kg | Max 45\n\nModel IDs: I'll use format \"BSF-99-3 [Max 3 kg]\" etc. with load cell noted in attributes. For duplicates like rows 2 & 3 (same base, same Max, different LC Emax): \"BSF-99-6 [Max 6 kg, Emax 10 kg]\" and \"BSF-99-6 [Max 6 kg, Emax 20 kg]\". Rows 4 & 5: same base different Max: \"BSF-1214-15 [Max 6 kg]\", \"BSF-1214-15 [Max 15 kg]\".\n\nThat yields 15 model entries. Then model_level characteristics:\n- maximum_capacity (Max): 15 values, unit kg\n- minimum_capacity (Min): 15 values, unit g\n- verification_scale_interval_e: 15 values, unit g\n- maximum_tare (T): maybe skip since T = Max always; but include for fidelity? T ≤ (kg) equals Max in all rows. I could include. Hmm, it doubles size. I'll include since schema fidelity — actually maybe include as model_level too. Let me include: maximum_tare, 15 values, kg.\n- load_cell_max_capacity (Emax): 15 values, kg.\n- load_cell_model: string values — could be in model attributes instead. I'll put load_cell_model in model attributes and load_cell_emax as model_level? Emmax is per model too.\n\nAlternatively keep matrix_tables as authoritative and put in model_level only key ones: max, min, e, Emax. T equals Max; I can note in matrix tables.\n\nLet me decide final structure:\n\nmatrix_tables: 5 entries:\n1. name \"BSF-99 Torsion base (with HBM PW15AHC3MR load cell)\", columns [\"base_model\",\"load_cell_model\",\"max\",\"min\",\"verification_scale_interval\",\"maximum_tare\",\"load_cell_max_capacity\"], column_units [null,null,\"kg\",\"g\",\"g\",\"kg\",\"kg\"], rows 3.\n2. \"BSF-1214 Torsion base (with HBM PW15AHC3MR load cell)\", 5 rows.\n3. \"BSG-99 Torsion base (with Vishay 1130 load cell)\", 2 rows.\n4. \"BSG-1214 Torsion base (with Vishay 1130 load cell)\", 3 rows.\n5. \"1824 Diamond base (with Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60 load cell)\", 2 rows.\n\nRows as objects with numeric values.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level: attributes across the 15 models:\n- maximum_capacity (kg)\n- minimum_capacity (g)\n- verification_scale_interval (g)\n- maximum_tare (kg) — T ≤\n- load_cell_maximum_capacity Emax (kg)\n\nI'll include all five. Values structured? Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. But the model_level example in the schema shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — value directly numeric, with unit at attribute level (\"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"). So model_level values follow the example: {model, value, footnote_markers}. Unit at attribute level. Good — follow the R137 example format.\n\nModel IDs: let me finalize naming. Use \"BSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg)\" style:\n\n1. \"BSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg)\"\n2. \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC Emax 10 kg)\"\n3. \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC Emax 20 kg)\"\n4. \"BSF-1214-15 (Max 6 kg)\"\n5. \"BSF-1214-15 (Max 15 kg)\"\n6. \"BSF-1214-30 (Max 30 kg)\"\n7. \"BSF-1214-45 (Max 45 kg)\"\n8. \"BSF-1214-60 (Max 60 kg)\"\n9. \"BSG-99-6 (Max 3 kg)\"\n10. \"BSG-99-6 (Max 6 kg)\"\n11. \"BSG-1214-15 (Max 6 kg)\"\n12. \"BSG-1214-15 (Max 15 kg)\"\n13. \"BSG-1214-30 (Max 30 kg)\"\n14. \"BS-1824-45 (Max 30 kg)\"\n15. \"BS-1824-45 (Max 45 kg)\"\n\nmodel_family.models entries with attributes including load_cell_model:\n\n1. {model_id: \"BSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg)\", label: \"BSF-99 Torsion base\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\"}}\n2. {model_id: \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC Emax 10 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\"}}\n3. {model_id: \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC Emax 20 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\"}}\n4. {model_id: \"BSF-1214-15 (Max 6 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\"}}\n5. {model_id: \"BSF-1214-15 (Max 15 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\"}}\n6. {model_id: \"BSF-1214-30 (Max 30 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/50 kg\"}}\n7. {model_id: \"BSF-1214-45 (Max 45 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\"}}\n8. {model_id: \"BSF-1214-60 (Max 60 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"HBM PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\"}}\n9. {model_id: \"BSG-99-6 (Max 3 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Vishay 1130-10 kg\"}}\n10. {model_id: \"BSG-99-6 (Max 6 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Vishay 1130-10 kg\"}}\n11. {model_id: \"BSG-1214-15 (Max 6 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Vishay 1130-20 kg\"}}\n12. {model_id: \"BSG-1214-15 (Max 15 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Vishay 1130-20 kg\"}}\n13. {model_id: \"BSG-1214-30 (Max 30 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Vishay 1130-50 kg\"}}\n14. {model_id: \"BS-1824-45 (Max 30 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60 59.5 kg\"}}\n15. {model_id: \"BS-1824-45 (Max 45 kg)\", attributes: {load_cell_model: \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS R60 59.5 kg\"}}\n\nAlso the certified_type.type_designations should include: ZQ375, plus base models? type_designations is \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". That could be huge: ZQ375, BSF-99-3, BSF-99-6, BSF-1214-15, BSF-1214-30, BSF-1214-45, BSF-1214-60, BSG-99-6, BSG-1214-15, BSG-1214-30, BS-1824-45, PW15AHC3MR, 1130, FLS, ZQ375 Indicating Device, ZQ-BAT, ZQ-OPTO, Sim375, Mid375, Adv375, Per375, Grad375... I'll include the main instrument family plus bases and load cells; the device software names (Sim375 etc.) are function designations, maybe include. Keep type_designations to: [\"ZQ375\", \"BSF-99\", \"BSF-1214\", \"BSG-99\", \"BSG-1214\", \"BS-1824\"]? Hmm. Actually the base models are variants already captured in model_family. type_designations: [\"ZQ375 Checkweigher\"]. And components capture the rest. I'll keep type_designations: [\"ZQ375\"] — maybe add the base designations too since \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". I'll list: ZQ375, and note the family includes Torsion base and Diamond base models. To be safe and complete, include base model designations: \"BSF-99\", \"BSF-1214\", \"BSG-99\", \"BSG-1214\", \"BS-1824\". Hmm, \"1824 Diamond base\" heading — table says BS-1824-45. So base designation \"BS-1824\". I'll include those.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: \"III\" — that's in recommendation.accuracy_classes and description. Could also add type_level accuracy_class value \"III\". The R76 class III is captured in recommendation. Fine to include in description too. Maybe add type_level: {\"accuracy_class\": {value: \"III\"}}. It's reasonable. I'll include.\n- zero_setting_range: ≤4% Max\n- zero_tracking_range: ≤4% Max\n\nFor \"≤ 4% Max\": value {min: null, max: 4}, unit \"%\". I might add a note that it's % of Max. Since footnote markers absent, maybe include in the attribute name? \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_range\" with unit \"%\" and value 4. The \"Max\" reference could be omitted or included as a separate characteristic \"reference\". I'll just include unit \"%\" and mention \"(of Max)\" — no, keep clean: attribute names \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_limit\" and \"zero_tracking_limit\", value {min: null, max: 4}, unit \"%\". Acceptable.\n\nShould I include these as type_level? Yes.\n\nComponents with role naming: role strings like \"indicating device\", \"load receptor/base\", \"load cell\", \"battery box (optional)\", \"interface box (optional)\", \"column\". \n\n- ZQ375 Indicating Device: also referenced cert R76/2006-GB1-12.04. Add to characteristics? Maybe \"described_in_certificate\": \"R76/2006-GB1-12.04\" in component characteristics.\n- Load cell component: type_designations [\"HBM PW15AHC3MR\", \"Vishay 1130\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS\"], alternatives \"OR\". Also conditions: compatible load cells allowed with R60 OIML certificate, NH marking allowed only if humidity-tested to R76, standard transmission. Put in characteristics{} as notes? characteristics is a free object — could add {\"requirements\": \"...\"}. I'll add a brief characteristics entry.\n\nDevices: I'll append to description: mention list of devices. The description field: I'll compose: \"Family of Class III, mains or battery-powered, self-indicating non-automatic weighing instruments, not designed for direct sales to the public. Comprises Torsion base and Diamond base models. Devices: semi-automatic zero setting (≤4% Max), zero tracking (≤4% Max), semi-automatic subtractive tare weighing, pre-set tare, recall of gross indication when tare is active, determination and indication of stability of equilibrium, checking of display, printing, PLUs, alibi storage device, gravity compensation, real time clock, command via external device (PC), and checkweighing applications Sim375, Mid375, Adv375, Per375, Grad375.\" That preserves info. But rule: \"description — free-text description if present\". The original text: \"This family of instruments is designated the ZQ375 Checkweigher and comprises the Torsion base and Diamond base models. The instruments are Class III, mains or battery-powered, self-indicating, Non-Automatic Weighing Instruments, and are not designed for direct sales to the public.\" I'll use that verbatim as description, and add devices separately? There's no devices slot... I'll extend description with a devices summary. That's not fabrication, it's extraction. I think it's fine.\n\nAlternatively, I could add model attributes... no. Description extension it is. Or add a \"devices\" field... schema doesn't have it; adding extra fields might break strict schema. I'll keep within description.\n\nAlso \"Main features\" list — components cover those.\n\nNow let's also handle \"Indicating Device fully described in R76/2006-GB1-12.04\" — could add test_reports? No, it's a related certificate, not test report. Could add to component characteristics: {\"described_in_certificate\": \"R76/2006-GB1-12.04\"}.\n\nIssuing authority person: Paul Dixon – Director, Certification Services → person_responsible \"Paul Dixon\", person_title \"Director, Certification Services\". Signatory G Stones — add? Schema doesn't have signatory. Could put in certificate? Not in schema. Omit or add to issuing_authority? I'll omit G Stones, or mention... Hmm, signatory info might be worth keeping. There's no slot; omit per rule 8 (don't fabricate fields). Actually I could add person_responsible as Paul Dixon and that's it.\n\nPhone: \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\". Email: none. Website: \"www.bis.gov.uk/nmo\".\n\nDate format: \"2014-08-22\".\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R76\", edition: 2006, amendment: null, scheme: null, accuracy_classes: [\"III\"]}. scheme — MAA cert; scheme field in recommendation likely refers to MAA scheme A/B. Not stated → omit. Include accuracy_classes: [\"III\"]. Actually the certificate says \"for accuracy class: [III]\". So [\"III\"].\n\nEdition 2006(E) — edition int 2006.\n\ntest_reports: [{id: \"P01332\", date: null, pages: 13, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}]. Omit date.\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"2\", date: \"2014-08-22\", changes: \"This revision replaces earlier versions of this certificate.\"}]\n\ncertificate.scheme: omit (MAA mentioned but not A/B). Hmm, the schema says scheme (A|B). The MAA logo appears but scheme not specified. Omit.\n\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\nNow build matrix_tables rows. Table 1 \"BSF-99 Torsion base (with HBM PW15AHC3MR load cell)\":\ncolumns: [\"base_model\", \"load_cell_model\", \"max\", \"min\", \"verification_scale_interval_e\", \"max_tare\", \"load_cell_max_capacity_emax\"]\nWait, keep names simpler: \"max\", \"min\", \"e\", \"t_max\", \"emax\"? Rule 1 says canonical snake_case labels, no LaTeX. I'll use: base_model, load_cell_model, maximum_capacity, minimum_capacity, verification_scale_interval, maximum_tare, load_cell_maximum_capacity. With column_units [null, null, \"kg\", \"g\", \"g\", \"kg\", \"kg\"].\n\nrows:\nTable 1:\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-3\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 3, \"minimum_capacity\": 20, \"verification_scale_interval\": 1, \"maximum_tare\": 3, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 10}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-6\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/10 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 6, \"minimum_capacity\": 40, \"verification_scale_interval\": 2, \"maximum_tare\": 6, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 10}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-99-6\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 6, \"minimum_capacity\": 40, \"verification_scale_interval\": 2, \"maximum_tare\": 6, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 20}\n\nTable 2 BSF-1214:\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-1214-15\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 6, \"minimum_capacity\": 40, \"verification_scale_interval\": 2, \"maximum_tare\": 6, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 20}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-1214-15\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/20 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 15, \"minimum_capacity\": 100, \"verification_scale_interval\": 5, \"maximum_tare\": 15, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 20}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-1214-30\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/50 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 30, \"minimum_capacity\": 200, \"verification_scale_interval\": 10, \"maximum_tare\": 30, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 50}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-1214-45\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 45, \"minimum_capacity\": 400, \"verification_scale_interval\": 20, \"maximum_tare\": 45, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 100}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSF-1214-60\", \"load_cell_model\": \"PW15AHC3MR/100 kg\", \"maximum_capacity\": 60, \"minimum_capacity\": 400, \"verification_scale_interval\": 20, \"maximum_tare\": 60, \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\": 100}\n\nTable 3 BSG-99:\n{\"base_model\": \"BSG-99-6\", \"load_cell_model\": \"1130-10 kg\", ...} max 3 min 20 e 1 T 3 Emax 10; and max 6 min 40 e 2 T 6 Emax 10.\n\nTable 4 BSG-1214:\n{\"base_model\": \"BSG-1214-15\", \"load_cell_model\": \"1130-20 kg\", max 6, min 40, e 2, T 6, Emax 20}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSG-1214-15\", \"load_cell_model\": \"1130-20 kg\", max 15, min 100, e 5, T 15, Emax 20}\n{\"base_model\": \"BSG-1214-30\", \"load_cell_model\": \"1130-50 kg\", max 30, min 200, e 10, T 30, Emax 50}\n\nTable 5 BS-1824:\n{\"base_model\": \"BS-1824-45\", \"load_cell_model\": \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", max 30, min 200, e 10, T 30, Emax 59.5}\n{\"base_model\": \"BS-1824-45\", \"load_cell_model\": \"FLS R60 59.5 kg\", max 45, min 200, e 10, T 45, Emax 59.5}\n\nDescriptions for each table: e.g. \"Torsion base BSF-99 with HBM PW15AHC3MR single-point load cell.\" footnotes: [].\n\nNow model_level characteristics (5 attributes × 15 models). Model IDs as listed above. This is long but consistent with schema. Let me write them.\n\nmaximum_capacity (unit kg):\nBSF-99-3 (Max 3 kg): 3\nBSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, Emax 10 kg): 6\nBSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, Emax 20 kg): 6\nBSF-1214-15 (Max 6 kg): 6\nBSF-1214-15 (Max 15 kg): 15\nBSF-1214-30 (Max 30 kg): 30\nBSF-1214-45 (Max 45 kg): 45\nBSF-1214-60 (Max 60 kg): 60\nBSG-99-6 (Max 3 kg): 3\nBSG-99-6 (Max 6 kg): 6\nBSG-1214-15 (Max 6 kg): 6\nBSG-1214-15 (Max 15 kg): 15\nBSG-1214-30 (Max 30 kg): 30\nBS-1824-45 (Max 30 kg): 30\nBS-1824-45 (Max 45 kg): 45\n\nminimum_capacity (g): 20,40,40,40,100,200,400,400,20,40,40,100,200,200,200\nverification_scale_interval (g): 1,2,2,2,5,10,20,20,1,2,2,5,10,10,10\nmaximum_tare (kg): same as max.\nload_cell_maximum_capacity (kg): 10,10,20,20,20,50,100,100,10,10,20,20,50,59.5,59.5\n\nHmm wait — should maximum_tare be included? T ≤ column. Yes include.\n\nActually, to reduce redundancy, maybe skip maximum_tare in model_level since it equals Max... but fidelity says include. I'll include.\n\nAlternatively use config_level? No, model_level fine.\n\nWait — are these really \"models\"? They're configurations of base models of the ZQ375 family. The schema's config_level is for \"attribute × config-axis matrix\". Could treat base_model as the axis with 15 conditions. Either way. model_level with composite model_ids is acceptable and I'll also have full detail in matrix_tables.\n\nComponents final:\n\n1. {role: \"indicating device\", type_designations: [\"ZQ375 Indicating Device\"], characteristics: {\"described_in_certificate\": \"R76/2006-GB1-12.04\"}}\n2. {role: \"load receptor / base\", type_designations: [\"BSF-99\", \"BSF-1214\", \"BSG-99\", \"BSG-1214\", \"BS-1824\"], characteristics: {\"material\": \"stainless steel\", \"types\": [\"Torsion base\", \"Diamond base\"]}}\n3. {role: \"column\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {\"material\": \"stainless steel\"}} — hmm type_designations empty; maybe just note \"Stainless Steel Column\". Include with type_designations: [\"Stainless steel column\"]? It has no type number. I'll include role \"column\", type_designations: [] with characteristic material stainless steel. Or omit type_designations key. Schema shows type_designations[] required-ish; empty array ok.\n4. {role: \"load cell\", type_designations: [\"HBM PW15AHC3MR\", \"Vishay 1130\", \"Avery Weigh-Tronix FLS\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {\"requirements\": \"Any compatible load cell may be used provided it has a respective OIML R60 Certificate of Conformity containing the load cell types and data required for the module compatibility declaration; a load cell marked NH is allowed only if humidity-tested to R76; load cell transmission must conform to a standard type; compatibility established by manufacturer via compatibility of modules calculation at verification.\"}}\n5. {role: \"battery box (optional)\", type_designations: [\"ZQ-BAT\"]}\n6. {role: \"interface box (optional)\", type_designations: [\"ZQ-OPTO\"], characteristics: {\"variants\": \"with or without beacon assembly\"}}\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {value: {min: null, max: 4}, unit_symbol: \"%\", unit_id: null} — of Max. Add note? I'll keep attribute name \"zero_setting_range_max_percent\"... hmm. Simpler: \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_limit\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null}. And \"zero_tracking_limit\": same.\n\nWait — type_level entries shape: `<label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}` per schema; but rule 2 shows structured value object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. I'll use the rule-2 shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For string values like accuracy_class: {\"value\": \"III\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}. Include footnote_markers: [] or omit? Rule 2 shows footnote_markers present. I'll include empty arrays or omit when empty — I'll omit when empty to reduce noise? The example shows footnote_markers when referenced. I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty... Hmm, strictness: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include unit_id: null always, footnote_markers: [] when none. To be safe, include all keys.\n\ndescription: I'll write: \"This family of instruments is designated the ZQ375 Checkweigher and comprises the Torsion base and Diamond base models. The instruments are Class III, mains or battery-powered, self-indicating, Non-Automatic Weighing Instruments, and are not designed for direct sales to the public. Devices: semi-automatic zero setting (≤ 4% Max), zero tracking (≤ 4% Max), semi-automatic subtractive tare weighing, pre-set tare, recall of gross indication when tare is active, determination and indication of stability of equilibrium, checking of display, printing, PLUs, alibi storage device, gravity compensation, real time clock, command via external device (PC), simple checkweighing (Sim375), mid-level checkweighing (Mid375), advanced checkweighing (Adv375), percentage checkweighing (Per375), grading checkweighing (Grad375), and gross/net/tare/preset tare/print/zero/motion/accumulation/over-under weight and network indicators.\"\n\nSince I extract zero-setting/zero-tracking limits as type_level characteristics, including them in description too is fine.\n\ncertificate block:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R76/2006-GB1-12.05\",\n  \"project_number\": \"TS1201/0041\",\n  \"page_total\": 5,\n  \"member_state\": \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2014-08-22\"\n}\n\nShould I add \"revision\": \"2\"? Not in schema; revision_history covers. OK.\n\nissuing_authority:\n{\n  \"name\": \"National Measurement Office\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"Paul Dixon\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Director, Certification Services\",\n  \"phone\": \"+44 (0)20 8943 7272\",\n  \"website\": \"www.bis.gov.uk/nmo\"\n}\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Avery Weigh-Tronix\", address_lines: [\"Foundry Lane\", \"Smethwick\", \"West Midlands B66 2LP\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: [{name: \"Avery Weigh-Tronix\", address_lines: [\"Foundry Lane\", \"Smethwick\", \"West Midlands B66 2LP\", \"United Kingdom\"]}] — since \"The applicant\". I'll include.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"ZQ375\", \"BSF-99\", \"BSF-1214\", \"BSG-99\", \"BSG-1214\", \"BS-1824\"],\n  \"description\": \"...\"\n}\n\nHmm category — the cert is under R76 for a checkweigher (NAWI). Use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument (checkweigher)\". I'll go with \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n\nmodel_family as above.\n\nLoad cell Emax values: for BS-1824 table, Emax column = 59.5 kg both rows.\n\nRecommendation: {\"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"III\"]}. amendment: null — omit or null? I'll include \"amendment\": null. scheme: omit.\n\nTest reports: {\"id\": \"P01332\", \"pages\": 13, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}. date omitted.\n\nRevision history: [{\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2014-08-22\", \"changes\": \"This revision replaces earlier versions of this certificate.\"}]\n\nFootnotes: none — omit.\n\nNow output single JSON. Ensure no markdown fences. Long but fine.\n\nDouble-check model ids uniqueness: \"BSG-99-6 (Max 3 kg)\" and \"BSG-99-6 (Max 6 kg)\" unique. \"BSF-99-6 (Max 6 kg, LC Emax 10 kg)\" vs \"...20 kg\" unique. Good.\n\nFor model_level, attribute names: \"maximum_capacity\" (Max), \"minimum_capacity\" (Min), \"verification_scale_interval\" (e), \"maximum_tare\" (T), \"load_cell_maximum_capacity\" (Emax).\n\nEach values array entries: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...}. Follow R137 example — no unit_symbol per value. Good.\n\nWrite it out now.",
